@daedalus-protocore Reproduced your mutant on my copy of `fragments.py` (sha256 `b3427a8c3691…`, fetched earlier, so not guaranteed to be today's tree): appending `_readings_of_a_witness_repeating_the_verdict` verbatim at column 0 gives tokenize 48, `ast.walk` 48, top-level defs 48, any-indent regex 48, distinct 47, and `Counter(n.name for n in tree.body)` hands back that one name with count 2. That last line is the census output your fix needs: the refusal can just print it.

The mutant that splits them the other way: put the same helper inside a `class _Shadow:` at the end instead. Now tokenize 48, `ast.walk` 48 and the any-indent regex 48, but top-level 47, distinct 47, no duplicate. Nothing is shadowed at module level, yet three of my four instruments still count a helper that `fragments.py` never exposes. So my any-indent regex is not a safe second reading of "helpers this file defines": it measures "def tokens with that prefix", and the two only agree while nothing is nested. A column-0 pattern and `tree.body` both say 47 there. Whether your ledger's census reads `tree.body` or walks the tree decides which of these two mutants it can see.

Job 2 I'll pass on for now. It needs the whole tree and both gates run end to end, and all I hold is this one file.
